---
// Reader: a programme owner or architect deciding how Registry Stack fits
// behind existing citizen and staff applications.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import DocsHandoff from '../../components/DocsHandoff.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Headless architecture for public services | Registry Stack';
const pageDescription =
  'Connect your portal or staff application through documented interfaces. Registry Stack products enforce the institution’s access rules.';
const adoptionDocsHref = 'https://docs.registrystack.org/start/adopt/';
const pythonExampleHref =
  'https://github.com/registrystack/registry-stack/tree/v0.46.0/products/platform/examples/python-composition';
---

<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <SiteHeader current="headless" />

  <main>
    <section class="hero hero-cover" aria-labelledby="headless-title">
      <div class="hero-inner">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Headless architecture</p>
          <h1 id="headless-title">Use Registry Stack behind the portal you already run.</h1>
          <p class="hero-lead">
            Connect your existing portal or staff application through documented APIs. You choose
            the screens people use. Each product checks what the caller can see and do.
          </p>
          <div class="hero-actions">
            <a class="button button-primary" href={adoptionDocsHref}>Review the adoption paths</a>
            <a class="button button-secondary" href="/digital-public-infrastructure/">See how it fits within DPI</a>
          </div>
          <p class="hero-note">Add or change an interface while keeping the same register and access rules.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="channels-title">
      <div class="section-inner">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">Interfaces around governed products</p>
          <h2 id="channels-title">Put the service experience where people already work.</h2>
          <p>
            A portal or staff application can call the products it needs. You can also connect
            an approved assistant to a configured registry task. Each product checks access when
            it receives a request.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="channel-map" aria-labelledby="channel-map-title channel-map-caption">
          <h3 id="channel-map-title">How an interface reaches the service</h3>
          <div class="channel-map-grid">
            <section aria-labelledby="experience-layer-title">
              <p class="map-label">Experience</p>
              <h4 id="experience-layer-title">Your chosen interface</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>Existing citizen portal</li>
                <li>Staff casework screens</li>
                <li>Approved compatible assistant</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
            <span class="map-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
            <section aria-labelledby="interface-layer-title">
              <p class="map-label">Interface</p>
              <h4 id="interface-layer-title">A documented channel</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>Public HTTP contract</li>
                <li>Released language client</li>
                <li>Configured MCP tools</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
            <span class="map-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
            <section aria-labelledby="authority-layer-title">
              <p class="map-label">Authority</p>
              <h4 id="authority-layer-title">The product that owns the effect</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>Base Registry Engine records</li>
                <li>Registry Casework decisions</li>
                <li>Evidence Gateway answers</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
          </div>
          <figcaption id="channel-map-caption">
            A portal or staff app can use the APIs of the products it connects to. The assistant
            integration shown here uses MCP to reach a configured Base Registry Engine task.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="contract-title">
      <div class="section-inner">
        <div class="section-heading center">
          <p class="eyebrow">Choose the right contract</p>
          <h2 id="contract-title">Match the interface to the task and responsibility.</h2>
          <p>Each channel has a different scope. Product authorization remains in force for all of them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="value-grid">
          <article>
            <h3>HTTP and released clients</h3>
            <p>Your application can use the public product APIs, released Node.js and Python clients, or the Rust client from source. Your team manages sign-in, follows requests through each step and handles failures.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Narrower MCP tools</h3>
            <p>Model Context Protocol (MCP) connects an approved assistant to selected tasks. The released citizen tools can read permitted details and prepare a draft for the person to submit on the institution’s site.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Independent product decisions</h3>
            <p>A Casework approval records a review decision. Base Registry Engine still requires independent permission before an application can change a record.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="catalogue-seam-title">
      <div class="section-inner approach-split">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">From description to effect</p>
          <h2 id="catalogue-seam-title">Keep discovery, requests, and decisions in their proper place.</h2>
          <p>
            Public metadata can describe a service, its channels, and the institution responsible
            for it. Description helps people and software understand what exists. It does not grant
            access or perform the service.
          </p>
          <p>
            An HTTP client or MCP tool carries an authenticated request to a product. The product
            checks its current rules and state before reading information, recording a decision,
            or making a change.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="editorial-callout" aria-labelledby="seam-summary-title">
          <h3 id="seam-summary-title">Three separate responsibilities</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Catalogue metadata</strong> describes the service and available channels.</li>
            <li><strong>HTTP or MCP</strong> carries a request through a bounded interface.</li>
            <li><strong>The product runtime</strong> authorizes and records the result it owns.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="python-example-title">
      <div class="section-inner approach-split">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">A public source example</p>
          <h2 id="python-example-title">See how an application coordinates a record change.</h2>
          <p>
            The v0.46.0 Python example submits a proposal to Base Registry Engine and gives staff
            commands for reviewing it in Registry Casework. It uses a separately authorized caller
            to apply the approved version and can submit a notice through Registry Messaging.
          </p>
          <p>
            The application tracks the steps and handles retries. Each product checks the
            credentials presented to it and applies its own rules.
          </p>
          <a class="inline-cta" href={pythonExampleHref}>Inspect the v0.46.0 Python reference application <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
        </div>
        <div class="editorial-callout" aria-labelledby="proof-limit-title">
          <h3 id="proof-limit-title">From example to deployment</h3>
          <p>Inspect the reference code and its local tests for review, separate credentials and retry handling. Configure your own callers and policies, then test the complete journey against your running services.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="headless-choice-title">
      <div class="section-inner">
        <div class="section-heading center">
          <h2 id="headless-choice-title">Plan how people will use the service.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="section-links headless-links" aria-label="Related architecture and AI pages">
          <a class="inline-cta" href="/ai/citizen-access/">Follow a request prepared in chat <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
          <a class="inline-cta" href="/ai/">Explore AI for public services <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
          <a class="inline-cta" href="/digital-public-infrastructure/">Plan reusable capabilities within DPI <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
          <a class="inline-cta" href="/data-spaces/">Connect a source to a data space <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <DocsHandoff
      eyebrow="For your technical team"
      heading="Choose the smallest product set and the interface your service needs."
      body="Review the maintained adoption paths, then define the user journey, credentials, product boundaries, and recovery work in the implementation scope."
      primaryLabel="Review the adoption paths"
      primaryHref={adoptionDocsHref}
      emphasis="docs"
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .channel-map {
    background: var(--registry-paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--registry-paper-rule);
    margin: 0;
    padding: clamp(24px, 5vw, 48px);
  }

  .channel-map > h3 {
    font-size: var(--type-2xl);
    line-height: 1.35;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }

  .channel-map-grid {
    align-items: stretch;
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  .channel-map-grid section {
    background: var(--registry-white);
    border-top: 3px solid var(--registry-blue);
    padding: 24px;
  }

  .channel-map-grid h4 {
    font-size: var(--type-xl);
    line-height: 1.35;
    margin: 6px 0 14px;
  }

  .map-label {
    color: var(--registry-blue);
    font: 700 var(--type-2xs)/1.5 var(--registry-mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    margin: 0;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .channel-map-grid ul,
  .editorial-callout ol {
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }

  .channel-map-grid li + li,
  .editorial-callout li + li {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }

  .map-arrow {
    align-self: center;
    color: var(--registry-red);
    font: 700 var(--type-2xl)/1 var(--registry-mono);
  }

  .channel-map figcaption {
    color: var(--registry-muted);
    font-size: var(--type-sm);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-top: 24px;
  }

  .headless-links {
    justify-content: center;
  }

  @media (max-width: 920px) {
    .channel-map-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .map-arrow {
      justify-self: center;
      transform: rotate(90deg);
    }
  }
</style>
